eady have a pair).  \n- Play B1: Leaves wait on W3 or W6 or J3. B1 has 3 left, moderately available.  \n\nConsidering you already have two W3, discarding one W3 would leave you with a pair of W3, making you tenpai waiting for W3 to complete the pair for the win. This is a single wait (worth 1 point) and is straightforward. Discarding other tiles would leave you without a pair, meaning you would need to draw a matching tile for one of your singles to form a pair, which is less direct and less likely.  \n\nReason: Discarding W3 gives you a definite tenpai with a single wait on W3, which has 4 tiles remaining unseen, giving a reasonable chance. It simplifies your hand and avoids complex decisions. Other discards would leave you needing to draw a specific tile to form a pair, reducing immediacy. Given the game state, securing tenpai with a clear wait is optimal.  \n\nAnswer: Play W3"}
